WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.632
Say you write a Deployment that asks for two copies of nginx.

2
00:00:06.833 --> 00:00:10.611
You run kubectl apply, against a cluster like this one.

3
00:00:10.611 --> 00:00:13.564
Interviewers love to ask what happens next.

4
00:00:13.564 --> 00:00:15.899
So let us follow it, step by step.

5
00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:16.746
Step one.

6
00:00:16.746 --> 00:00:23.345
kubectl reads the config file in your home folder, to find the cluster and your credentials.

7
00:00:23.567 --> 00:00:27.968
Then it sends the YAML to the API server, over HTTPS.

8
00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:28.828
Step two.

9
00:00:28.828 --> 00:00:33.227
The API server checks who you are, and whether you may do this.

10
00:00:33.433 --> 00:00:38.411
It validates the object, and stores it in etcd, the cluster's database.

11
00:00:39.633 --> 00:00:43.032
Then it answers created, and kubectl exits.

12
00:00:43.233 --> 00:00:44.944
That took under a second.

13
00:00:44.944 --> 00:00:46.449
But look at the nodes.

14
00:00:46.449 --> 00:00:48.022
Nothing runs there yet.

15
00:00:48.022 --> 00:00:49.937
Your request is only stored.

16
00:00:51.367 --> 00:00:52.162
Step three.

17
00:00:52.162 --> 00:00:55.704
The Deployment controller watches the API server.

18
00:00:55.704 --> 00:00:57.728
A watch is one long request.

19
00:00:57.728 --> 00:01:00.908
The API server streams every change down it.

20
00:01:02.533 --> 00:01:05.922
It sees the new Deployment, and creates a ReplicaSet.

21
00:01:05.922 --> 00:01:10.141
That is a helper object that keeps a set number of identical Pods.

22
00:01:11.767 --> 00:01:12.468
Step four.

23
00:01:12.468 --> 00:01:17.732
The ReplicaSet controller sees the ReplicaSet, and creates two Pod objects.

24
00:01:20.333 --> 00:01:21.983
They have no node yet.

25
00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:22.939
Step five.

26
00:01:22.939 --> 00:01:26.117
The scheduler sees two Pods without a node.

27
00:01:27.233 --> 00:01:29.971
It filters out nodes that cannot take them.

28
00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:31.679
It scores the rest.

29
00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:34.253
And it writes a node name into each Pod.

30
00:01:34.967 --> 00:01:35.633
Step six.

31
00:01:35.633 --> 00:01:39.929
The kubelet on each chosen node sees a Pod assigned to it.

32
00:01:41.533 --> 00:01:45.551
It asks containerd to pull the image, and start the container.

33
00:01:46.967 --> 00:01:47.764
Step seven.

34
00:01:47.764 --> 00:01:49.575
The kubelet reports back.

35
00:01:49.575 --> 00:01:52.981
The Pod is running, and here is its IP address.

36
00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:56.257
The API server stores that in the Pod's status.

37
00:01:56.467 --> 00:01:58.485
Now look at who talked to whom.

38
00:01:58.485 --> 00:02:01.024
Every step went through the API server.

39
00:02:01.233 --> 00:02:03.166
No part sent orders to another.

40
00:02:03.166 --> 00:02:05.535
Each one acted on what it found there.

41
00:02:06.967 --> 00:02:10.440
Each step also leaves a short record, called an event.

42
00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:13.721
kubectl get events shows the whole chain, in order.

43
00:02:13.933 --> 00:02:17.100
If a step cannot happen, the chain stops there.

44
00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:21.143
A Pod that fits on no node stays Pending, with no node name.

45
00:02:21.143 --> 00:02:22.423
Its events say why.

46
00:02:22.633 --> 00:02:25.196
So kubectl apply only stores your wish.

47
00:02:25.196 --> 00:02:30.519
The controllers, the scheduler and the kubelet make it real, a few seconds later.
